1
00:00:08,349 --> 00:00:16,649
Twenty-four centuries ago, Plato told of a king who was offered a marvelous new invention: writing.

2
00:00:17,149 --> 00:00:30,035
The king refused. This, he warned, gives only the appearance of wisdom — while the living capacity to question, to explain, and to judge quietly fades.

3
00:00:30,535 --> 00:00:40,382
He was wrong to fear it — but right to ask. Every tool since has carried his question: who is doing the thinking?

4
00:00:40,882 --> 00:00:46,771
Writing was only the first. It remembers, so we don't have to.

5
00:00:47,271 --> 00:00:51,378
Calculators compute in an instant.

6
00:00:51,878 --> 00:00:56,114
And search retrieves the world's answers.

7
00:00:56,614 --> 00:01:14,396
Generative AI is different: it can frame the problem, build the argument, and judge the answer — the very things you set out to learn. When a tool does the part you were meant to practice, help quietly becomes replacement.

8
00:01:14,896 --> 00:01:20,103
P-FACET is a routine for keeping the thinking yours.

9
00:01:20,603 --> 00:01:28,508
P — Problem. Anchor your work in something real: what am I solving, and why does it matter?

10
00:01:29,008 --> 00:01:36,251
F — Frame. Decide what good looks like: your goals, your criteria, your constraints.

11
00:01:36,751 --> 00:01:46,459
A — Analogize and Attempt. Connect what you already know — and try it yourself first. That draft is yours.

12
00:01:46,959 --> 00:02:00,731
C — Consult and Construct. Now, and only now, bring in AI. Ask it to critique, to offer alternatives, to poke holes — while you keep building. Not to write for you.

13
00:02:01,231 --> 00:02:09,968
E — Evaluate and Explain. Check what it gave you. What do you keep, what do you drop — and why?

14
00:02:10,468 --> 00:02:17,957
T — Transform and Transfer. Make the work yours, and carry the routine to your next task.

15
00:02:18,457 --> 00:02:25,690
Notice: six phases — and AI enters directly at exactly one.

16
00:02:26,190 --> 00:02:32,591
Here's what it looks like. A student working on campus food waste starts by framing her goal.

17
00:02:33,091 --> 00:02:40,185
Then she makes her own attempt — a recommendation drafted from her own evidence.

18
00:02:40,685 --> 00:02:49,763
Only now does she consult AI: Challenge my reasoning. Give me two counterarguments — don't write my conclusion.

19
00:02:50,263 --> 00:02:58,243
She evaluates what comes back — rejecting one claim for weak evidence, revising another after checking the source.

20
00:02:58,743 --> 00:03:04,589
And she transforms the work, noting what she'll carry into next time.

21
00:03:05,089 --> 00:03:17,122
Your thinking leaves a trail: your first attempt, your bounded prompts, your reasons for keeping or cutting. That trail shows your learning — instead of hiding it.

22
00:03:17,622 --> 00:03:33,751
So the next time AI helps you, ask one question: who did the thinking? P-FACET keeps the answer — you. Try the three-minute Quick Start, and run your next assignment through it.

